    "answer": "**Episode\u202f7 \u2013 \u201cOn Computable Numbers, Turing\u202f1936\u201d** is the one that talks about the fundamental limits of what a computer (or any mechanical procedure) can decide. In the excerpt the speaker explains Turing\u2019s diagonal\u2011argument proof that there are far more real numbers than there are machines to compute them, so most real numbers are uncomputable and no machine can list them all\u202f\u2014\u202fthe very essence of a decision\u2011making limit for computers (Episode\u202f7, 16:05\u201117:35).\n\nThe other episodes you listed focus on different topics:\n\n* **Episode\u202f3** discusses the race to model DNA and the dangers of publishing a wrong structural model.  \n* **Episode\u202f4** explains Shannon\u2019s existence proof for good error\u2011correcting codes, not computational decidability.  \n* **Episode\u202f5** critiques the memory and parallelism problems of early recurrent neural networks.\n\nNone of those episodes address the limits of computability, so Episode\u202f7 is your best bet. Happy listening!",
